WEBVTT

cue-VID_U5_03-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Picketline Fencing Co installs garden fences, but its owner cannot explain where the money went. Sales were busy all year, yet the bank balance barely moved. An Income Statement records income and all costs incurred to earn that income over a period, usually a year. It is built vertically, line by line, from revenue down to retained profit. Watch each figure drop into place like a fence post.

cue-VID_U5_03-2
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Start at the top with revenue. Revenue is the income from selling goods or services during a period. Picketline billed one hundred twenty thousand dollars for fencing jobs last year. Cost of sales is the cost of the posts, rails, concrete and gates actually used on those jobs: forty-five thousand dollars. Subtract cost of sales from revenue. One hundred twenty thousand minus forty-five thousand gives gross profit of seventy-five thousand dollars.

cue-VID_U5_03-3
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Gross profit is made when revenue is greater than cost of sales. It shows the margin earned before overheads. Now subtract expenses, the fixed costs and overheads of the period. Picketline spent thirty-eight thousand dollars on fitter wages, van fuel, insurance and yard rent. Seventy-five thousand minus thirty-eight thousand leaves profit of thirty-seven thousand dollars. Profit is total income less total costs, and it rewards the owner for risk-taking.

cue-VID_U5_03-4
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Profit is not the same as cash in the owner's pocket. First deduct tax of seven thousand dollars. Thirty-seven thousand minus seven thousand leaves thirty thousand dollars. Then pay dividends of ten thousand dollars to the shareholders. Thirty thousand minus ten thousand leaves retained profit of twenty thousand dollars. Retained profit is the earnings kept in the business for growth, perhaps a second van or a wider stock of fencing materials.

cue-VID_U5_03-5
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Why does profit matter? It is a reward for risk-taking and a source of finance for expansion. It measures success and attracts investors and lenders. Because retained profit can fund a new van, therefore the firm borrows less, so future interest costs fall. Comparing this Income Statement with last year's reveals whether material costs or overheads are rising. A falling profit warns the owner before the problem becomes a crisis.

cue-VID_U5_03-6
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Do not confuse revenue with profit. Revenue is the total sales income: one hundred twenty thousand dollars. Profit is what remains after costs: thirty-seven thousand dollars. On screen, the revenue bar stretches across the frame while the profit bar is only a fraction as long. A firm can grow revenue and still see profit shrink if costs rise faster. Always show the calculation in two steps: revenue minus cost of sales, then minus expenses.

cue-VID_U5_03-7
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Now evaluate what profit does and does not show. It shows whether trading earned more than it cost, and it helps compare years. It does not show cash timing, so a profitable firm can still miss a supplier payment. It does not show the value of assets, the quality of fences or customer goodwill. Historic figures look backward. Use the Income Statement beside a cash-flow forecast and a statement of financial position before deciding.

cue-VID_U5_03-8
00:03:28.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Let us rebuild the whole vertical statement in one pass. Revenue one hundred twenty thousand; cost of sales forty-five thousand; gross profit seventy-five thousand; expenses thirty-eight thousand; profit thirty-seven thousand; tax seven thousand; dividends ten thousand; retained profit twenty thousand. Each row locks into place like a completed panel. The advantage is clarity; the limitation is that one period never tells the whole story of a fencing business. Compare several years before judging performance.

